INT. FERRY GALLEY - NIGHT

A cramped galley below decks, steel counters and swinging pots. The boat creaks. A single porthole shows black water.

ESTELLE MARCHAND (50s, elegant, wearing her good coat in a kitchen) sits at the narrow table, stirring a cup of cold tea. ARTHUR PENHALLOW (60s, ship's cook, apron, forearms like rope) stands at the stove with his back to her, ladling soup.

ESTELLE
You might have told me.

ARTHUR
I would have.

ESTELLE
When?

ARTHUR
When it was true.

She sets the spoon down. He stirs the pot. The ferry rocks.

ESTELLE
It has been true for a year.

ARTHUR
It has been possible for a year.

ESTELLE
Arthur.

ARTHUR
Estelle.

The galley door swings open. TILDA VANCE (20s, deckhand, oilskin, red hands) comes in with a bucket and stops short.

TILDA
Sorry. I can come back.

ARTHUR
Stay. You'll want soup.

ESTELLE
Do stay, dear. We were only discussing the weather.

TILDA
It's bad out.

ARTHUR
It is.

ESTELLE
It's worse in here.

Tilda looks from one to the other. She sets down the bucket.

TILDA
Should I--

ARTHUR
Take a bowl.

ESTELLE
Take two.

TILDA
Okay.

ARTHUR
(to Estelle)
You could have written.

ESTELLE
(to Arthur)
You could have answered.

TILDA
(halfway through a mouthful)
This is really good soup.

Arthur and Estelle turn together and look at her. Tilda sets down the spoon.

TILDA (CONT'D)
I'll just--

ARTHUR
Eat.

ESTELLE
Eat, child.

She does. The ferry groans in the swell, and somewhere above them a horn sounds, long and low, for a harbor they cannot see.
